Journal: one file per session, appended in place
================================================

Input is recorded at send time, the reply when the turn completes, into one append-only file per
session. Both backends write the same format, so a session recorded by the desktop app is readable by
the Hermes-side hooks and vice versa.

WHY APPEND AND NOT REWRITE

The design this replaces — rewriting the session file each turn — costs O(n^2) bytes written for a
linear amount of content, because turn n writes everything written so far again. That is the memory
hole. Measured on 2000 turns of realistic sizes in the standalone twin of this module
(~/Projects/palace-journal):

    final file on disk      1.49 MiB
    per turn                782 bytes (input + output)
    bytes the writer wrote  equal to the final size — nothing rewritten
    rewrite-per-turn        1.46 GiB for the same session
    difference              999x
    a year at 3/day         1.6 GiB

GROWTH IS LINEAR, NOT "51 RECORDS < 50x ONE RECORD"

The test for this was written wrong the first time. Comparing 51 records against 50x a single record
is false for a correct implementation, because the per-record header (~70 bytes) dominates a small
body. The property that actually distinguishes linear from quadratic is that each block of records
costs the same: a rewrite-per-turn design makes the second half of a session about three times the
first. The test measures that ratio and requires it to be within 10% of 1.

WHAT IT REFUSES TO DO

- It never fails a turn. Every entry point returns () and swallows its own errors; a journal that
  breaks a conversation is worse than no journal. The Hermes hooks do the same and are verified to
  exit 0 on malformed JSON, empty stdin, a non-object payload, an empty body, and a missing binary.
- It does not fsync. Flush only. fsync per turn is a measurable stall for a safety net.
- It does not curate. This is a journal: verbatim, dumb, cheap. Promoting entries into palace drawers
  is a separate pass and must not live in the hot path.
- It does not escape the journal directory: the session id is sanitised, and a test asserts that
  "../../etc/passwd" as a session id still lands inside the journal dir.

THE PAIR IS THE POINT

An input alone says a question was asked. Input plus output says what was answered, and lets the same
question asked months apart be compared after a model changes underneath. Outputs carry the model id
and the turn duration that the engine already computed, so the number in the journal is the same
number the UI shows rather than a second clock that could disagree.

